<body data-view="both">

<header class="hero">
  <span class="eyebrow">Bilingual Explainer · 双语科普</span>
  <h1>
    Why Is the Sky Blue?
    <span class="zh">天空为什么是蓝色的？</span>
  </h1>
  <p class="lede">The same fact — the sky is blue — explained at three different levels. 同一个事实，用三种不同的层次来解释。</p>
</header>

<nav class="controls" aria-label="View controls">
  <div class="group" data-label="View">
    <button data-view="both" class="active" aria-pressed="true">Side by side</button>
    <button data-view="en" aria-pressed="false">English</button>
    <button data-view="zh" aria-pressed="false">中文</button>
  </div>
  <div class="group" data-label="Theme">
    <button data-theme="light" class="active" aria-pressed="true">Light</button>
    <button data-theme="dark" aria-pressed="false">Dark</button>
  </div>
</nav>

<main>

  <!-- ================= Toddler ================= -->
  <section class="block" id="toddler">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="num">1</span>
      <h2>For a 3-Year-Old<span class="zh">给 3 岁的小朋友 · The Magic Blue Sky</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="col en">
        <span class="tag">EN</span>
        <p><strong>The sky is blue because of tiny things floating in the air that “scatter” light.</strong></p>
        <p>Imagine sunlight is a big crayon box of many colors — red, orange, yellow, green, blue. The sun’s light is like all the crayons in the box!</p>
        <p>Now, in the air, there are <em>tiny</em> invisible particles — like little specks or dust and water droplets. When the sunlight goes flying through the air, it bumps into these tiny things. The bumping is called “scattering” — like how a flashlight beam looks all spread out when you shine it through fog.</p>
        <p>And here’s the cool part: the tiny things bump the <em>blue</em> crayons around the most! The blue crayons get tossed in every direction, so no matter where you look, the sky has those blue crayons everywhere. That’s why the whole sky looks blue.</p>
        <p>The red crayons don’t bump as much, so they travel further and further through the air — that’s why the sky can look red or orange when the sun is low and big in the sky!</p>
        <p>So: <strong>tiny air specks + sunlight = a blue sky.</strong> Easy as 1, 2, 3!</p>
      </article>
      <article class="col zh">
        <span class="tag">中文</span>
        <p><strong>天空是蓝色的，是因为空气中漂浮着一些极小的微粒，把光“吹散”了。</strong></p>
        <p>想象一下，阳光是一大盒涂色蜡笔，里面有很多颜色——红色、橙色、黄色、绿色、蓝色。太阳的光就像盒子里全部蜡笔发出的光！</p>
        <p>现在，空气中有肉眼看不见的极小的微粒——像小小的尘埃、灰尘和水滴。当阳光飞过空气时，它会碰到这些极小的小东西。这种“碰撞”叫做“散射”——就像手电筒的光束穿过厚雾时，光像所有方面都散开一样。</p>
        <p>这里还有个最酷的地方：这些极小的小东西把 <em>蓝色</em> 的蜡笔“碰撞”得最多！蓝色蜡笔被弹向了各个方向，所以无论你看哪一边，天空里都有蓝色蜡笔的痕迹。这就是为什么整个天空看起来是蓝色的。</p>
        <p>红色蜡笔被碰撞得较少，所以能在空气中走得更远——这就是为什么太阳低低地、慢慢升起或落下的时候，天空会变成红色或橙色的原因！</p>
        <p>所以：<strong>空气中的极小颗粒 + 阳光 = 蓝色的天空。</strong>简单到 1、2、3！</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= High school ================= -->
  <section class="block" id="teen">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="num">2</span>
      <h2>For a High School Teenager<span class="zh">给高中生 · 分子与光波的关系</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="col en">
        <span class="tag">EN</span>
        <p><strong>The sky is blue because of Rayleigh scattering, and it’s all about the size of the particles compared to the wavelength of light.</strong></p>
        <p>Air is mostly nitrogen and oxygen (~78% and ~21%), with molecules about 0.3 nanometers across — vastly smaller than the wavelength of visible light (~400–700 nm). Light scatters off particles that are much smaller than its wavelength in a process called Rayleigh scattering.</p>
        <h3>The key physics</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Scattering intensity is proportional to 1/λ⁴.</strong> Blue light (~450 nm) scatters roughly 5–6 times more strongly than red light (~650 nm).</li>
          <li>When sunlight enters the atmosphere, short-wavelength (blue/violet) photons get scattered in all directions. Since blue scatters so strongly, even looking away from the sun, we see lots of blue light from all over the sky.</li>
          <li>Violet light scatters even more strongly (λ ≈ 400 nm), but the Sun emits less violet than blue, and our eyes are less sensitive to violet — so the net result perceived by humans is blue.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Other things worth knowing</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Not clouds:</strong> cloud particles (water droplets, ~10–30 μm) are much larger than the wavelength of light, so they scatter all wavelengths roughly equally — that’s why clouds appear white.</li>
          <li><strong>The horizon:</strong> looking near the horizon, light passes through more air, so more red light is scattered out and the sky gets paler/whiter.</li>
          <li><strong>Sunsets:</strong> at dawn and dusk, sunlight travels through a much longer path of air, scattering away most blue light before it reaches your eye. The remaining light is red and orange — which is also why the sun itself looks red.</li>
          <li>That’s why the sky on Mars looks butterscotch/pink — its atmosphere is thin and dusty, scattering in different ways.</li>
          <li><strong>Flight fact:</strong> on a clear day, the sky is blue because Rayleigh scattering is <em>unevenly</em> strong — quite strong but not total — which is why the sun still shines through it.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="col zh">
        <span class="tag">中文</span>
        <p><strong>天空是蓝色的，是因为瑞利散射（Rayleigh Scattering），关键在于颗粒有多大，以及和光的波长比较。</strong></p>
        <p>空气主要由氮气和氧气组成（约 78% 和 21%），分子直径约为 0.3 纳米——远小于可见光的波长（约 400–700 纳米）。当光遇到比它波长小得多的颗粒时，就会发生瑞利散射。</p>
        <h3>关键的物理</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>散射强度与 1/λ⁴ 成正比。</strong> 蓝色光（约 450 纳米）的散射强度大约比红色光（约 650 纳米）强 5–6 倍。</li>
          <li>当阳光进入大气层，波长较短的（蓝色/紫色）光子会被朝各个方向散射开。由于蓝色光散射得非常强，即使你把眼睛离太阳看开，也会看到天空各处都有蓝色光。</li>
          <li>紫色光的散射更强（λ ≈ 400 纳米），但太阳发出的紫色光比蓝色光更少，而且人眼对紫色的敏感度更低，所以感知到的整体效果是蓝色。</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>还有一些值得知道的事情</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>不是云：</strong> 云里的颗粒（水滴，约 10–30 微米）比光的波长大得多，所以它们对各种波长的光散射差不多一样——这就是云看起来是白色的原因。</li>
          <li><strong>地平线附近：</strong> 朝着地平线看，光线穿过的空气更多，更多的红色光被散射走了，所以天空会变得更浅、更白。</li>
          <li><strong>黄昏：</strong> 在黎明和黄昏时分，阳光穿过的空气路径大得多，绝大部分蓝色光在到达你眼睛之前就被散射掉了。剩下的光是红色的和橙色的——这也是为什么太阳本身看起来是红色的。</li>
          <li>这就是为什么火星的天空是牛奶巧克力色/粉色的——它的大气层很薄，而且充满灰尘，散射的方式也不同。</li>
          <li><strong>飞行的一个小知识：</strong> 在晴朗的日子里，天空是蓝色的，因为瑞利散射“不均匀”地强——不是特别强也不是特别弱，正是这种“中等偏强”的特征，太阳的光才还是能透过天空照进来。</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= PhD ================= -->
  <section class="block" id="phd">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="num">3</span>
      <h2>For a Physics PhD<span class="zh">给物理博士 · Rayleigh 散射与大气光学</span></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      <article class="col en">
        <span class="tag">EN</span>

        <h3>The basic picture</h3>
        <p><strong>Rayleigh scattering by molecules in the atmosphere explains the blue sky.</strong></p>
        <p>Visible sunlight (roughly 400–700 nm, peaking near ~500 nm in spectral radiance; the Sun’s spectrum peaks in the visible green) propagates through the troposphere, which is essentially a dilute gas of N₂ and O₂ molecules. Scattering is described by the dipole model of molecular polarization.</p>
        <p>A molecule with polarizability α, driven by an incident field <strong>E</strong>₀, acquires an induced dipole <strong>p</strong> = α<strong>E</strong>₀ radiating as a dipole. The dipole radiation (a dipole oriented along the incident polarization, averaged over polarizations) has intensity:</p>
        <span class="formula">I(θ) ∝ [ (1 + cos²θ) / 2 ] · 1/λ⁴</span>
        <p>where θ is the angle between the incident light and the scattered direction. The λ⁻⁴ dependence is the essential point: blue light (~450 nm) scatters much more strongly than red (~650 nm), by a factor of (650/450)⁴ ≈ 4–6 (the classic “(700/400)⁴ ≈ 9” figure for the full visible range).</p>

        <h3>Why blue (not violet)?</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>The Sun’s spectral output falls off steeply at short wavelengths — violet (~400 nm) is much weaker than blue (~470 nm) in the solar spectrum.</li>
          <li>The human eye’s sensitivity plummets below ~450 nm (the photopic curve rises sharply from the violet edge).</li>
          <li>The combination of these, weighted by the 1/λ⁴ scattering law and our visual response, peaks around 470–480 nm — perceived as blue.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Atmospheric geometry and path length</h3>
        <p>Observed skylight comes from scattering events at all altitudes along your line of sight, integrated over the air mass. Looking at the overhead sky, light traverses ~1 air mass; toward the horizon, the path length grows (sec χ, where χ is the zenith angle, and effectively diverging near the horizon for a plane-parallel atmosphere). Consequently:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Sky near the horizon is paler/whiter — more total scattering removes most of the blue, and large-angle multiple scattering mixes wavelengths; forward scattering shows up as a bright spot near the sun.</li>
          <li>On a clear day, zenith radiance and horizon radiance differ by a large factor of the scattering terms.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Color and directional effects</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>The scattered light is polarized: looking at 90° to the sun, the sky is strongly (~70–90%) linearly polarized with the electric field perpendicular to the scattering plane. This is the basis of polarization-based “skylight” navigation.</li>
          <li>At sunset and sunrise, the elevated path length of air (thousands of times longer than the tropospheric scale height) removes almost all blue: remaining direct light is red/orange, and the sky takes on a reddish/pink band near the horizon.</li>
          <li>Ozone in the stratosphere absorbs weakly in the Chappuis (orange/red) and Hartley (UV) bands; its Chappuis absorption contributes a small violet/teal tinge to high-altitude sky and is the cause of the deep blue of the sky in stratospheric haze. (This is a minor correction to the canonical white-light Rayleigh story.)</li>
          <li>Real-world sky color is also modified by aerosols (clouds, haze, dust): larger particles (r ≫ λ, e.g., 10–30 μm droplets) scatter via Mie scattering, which is roughly wavelength-independent across the visible — hence white/gray clouds.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Summary</h3>
        <div class="summary">
          <p>The blue sky is the aggregate of millions of Rayleigh-scattering events from air molecules, each depositing a λ⁻⁴-weighted fraction of the solar spectrum into every direction. Blue and violet scatter most strongly, but the solar spectrum is weak in the violet and our eyes are insensitive to it, so the net perceived color is blue. Geometry (path length), solar spectral shape, and eye sensitivity set the exact tone; aerosols, ozone, and clouds modify it locally.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="col zh">
        <span class="tag">中文</span>

        <h3>基本图像</h3>
        <p><strong>大气中的分子瑞利散射解释了天空的蓝色。</strong></p>
        <p>可见阳光（约 400–700 纳米，在光谱辐射度上峰值约在 500 纳米附近；太阳光谱在可见绿光处达到峰值）沿着大气层传播，而大气层（对流层）本质上是一种由 N₂ 和 O₂ 分子构成的稀薄气体。散射由分子极化的偶极子模型（dipole model）来描述。</p>
        <p>当一个极化率为 α 的分子受到入射场 <strong>E</strong>₀ 驱动时，会获得一个感生偶极子 <strong>p</strong> = α<strong>E</strong>₀，并作为偶极子辐射出去。偶极辐射（取偶极子沿入射偏振方向的情形并对偏振方向平均）的强度为：</p>
        <span class="formula">I(θ) ∝ [ (1 + cos²θ) / 2 ] · 1/λ⁴</span>
        <p>其中 θ 是入射光与散射方向之间的夹角。λ⁻⁴ 的依赖关系是关键所在：蓝色光（约 450 纳米）比红色光（约 650 纳米）散射得强得多，强约 (650/450)⁴ ≈ 4–6 倍（经典的全可见范围估计值是 “(700/400)⁴ ≈ 9”）。</p>

        <h3>为什么是蓝色（而不是紫色）？</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>太阳在短波长下的辐射输出迅速衰减——紫色（约 400 纳米）的强度远低于蓝色（约 470 纳米）。</li>
          <li>人眼在约 450 纳米以下的灵敏度急剧下降（明视觉曲线从紫边急剧上升）。</li>
          <li>把这两者与 1/λ⁴ 的散射定律和人眼视觉响应结合起来，结果峰值在约 470–480 纳米处——被我们感知为蓝色。</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>大气几何与光程</h3>
        <p>观测到的天空光来自你视线范围内所有高度的散射事件的叠加，并在整个大气路径上积分。当你看向天顶时，光线穿过约 1 个大气柱——即约 1 个大气质量单位（air mass）——；朝向地平线时，路径长度会增长（即按 sec χ 增长，这里 χ 是天顶角，对于平面平行的大气模型，在地平线附近路径长度近乎发散）。因此：</p>
        <ul>
          <li>地平线附近的天空更浅、更白——更多的总散射把大部分蓝色光移走了，大角度的多重散射把各种波长混合在一起；正向散射则表现为太阳附近的一个亮斑。</li>
          <li>在晴朗的日子里，天顶处的辐射亮度与地平线处的辐射亮度之间相差很大。</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>颜色与方向效应</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>散射光是偏振光：当你看着太阳的 90° 方向时，天空被强烈（约 70–90%）线性偏振，电场垂直于散射面。这就是基于偏振的“天空导航”（skylight navigation）的物理基础。</li>
          <li>在日出和日落时，空气路径长度大大增加（比对流层气柱高度大数千倍），几乎所有的蓝色光都被移走了：剩下的直射光是红色和橙色的，地平线附近的天空也呈现出红色或粉色的带。</li>
          <li>平流层臭氧在查普伊斯带（Chappuis，橙色/红色）和哈特利带（Hartley，紫外）弱吸收；其查普伊斯吸收给高空天空带来一点点紫色/青色的调子，也是平流层霾中深蓝天空的原因之一。（这是对经典白光瑞利散射故事的一次微小修正。）</li>
          <li>现实世界中，天空的颜色还会被气溶胶（云、霾、尘埃）所改变：尺寸更大的粒子（r ≫ λ，例如 10–30 微米的水滴）通过 Mie 散射，其散射强度在可见光范围内大致与波长无关——这就是云显得白色或灰色的原因。</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>总结</h3>
        <div class="summary">
          <p>蓝色天空是空气分子中数百万次瑞利散射事件的总和，每一次都把阳光光谱中占据 λ⁻⁴ 权重的那部分能量分发到各个方向。蓝色和紫色散射得最强，但太阳在紫色区的输出很弱，而且我们的眼睛对它不敏感，所以我们综合感知到的颜色是蓝色。几何结构（光路径长度）、太阳的光谱形状和人眼的灵敏度共同决定了精确的色调；气溶胶、臭氧和云则局部地改变了它。</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>

